"Internal Error ()\nciteturn8view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12101506/fullTextXML\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC12101506/fullTextXML is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn8view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10511643/?format=pubmed\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10511643/?format=pubmed is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - A&P_revised.doc (https://wolpertlab.neuroscience.columbia.edu/sites/default/files/content/papers/BayWol08.pdf)\nciteturn8view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://wolpertlab.neuroscience.columbia.edu/sites/default/files/content/papers/BayWol08.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 725\nL0@P0: Predictive attenuation in the perception of touch\nL1@P0: Paul M Bays1 & Daniel M Wolpert2\nL2@P0: 1\nL3@P0: Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience, University College London, 17 Queen Square,\nL4@P0: London, WC1N 3AR, UK\nL5@P0: 2\nL6@P0: Department of Engineering, University of Cambridge, Trumpington Street,\nL7@P0: Cambridge, CB2 1PZ, UK\nL8@P0: Chapter 16, pp 339-358\nL9@P0: Attention & Performance XXII, Sensorimotor Foundations of Higher Cognition\nL10@P0-1: Eds. Patrick Haggard, Yves Rosetti and Mitsuo Kawato (Nov 2007) The ability to distinguish sensations resulting from our own actions from\nL11@P1: those with an external cause is a fundamental aspect of human behaviour.\nL12@P1: This distinction is in some cases reflected directly in perception: for\nL13@P1: example, tickling oneself produces a less intense sensation than being\nL14@P1: tickled by someone else. In this chapter we review the evidence for a\nL15@P1: general process of sensory filtering that attenuates self-generated tactile\nL16@P1: sensation. This process depends upon a temporally-precise prediction of\nL17@P1: the sensory consequences of one’s actions. We demonstrate experimentally\nL18@P1: that tactile attenuation specifically affects self-generated sensory input,\nL19@P1: leaving unchanged externally-generated sensations in the same part of the\nL20@P1: body. The level of attenuation does not vary with stimulus intensity, but\nL21@P1: rather consists of a constant subtraction in the perceived intensity of the\nL22@P1: self-generated stimulus. However, the level of attenuation can be reduced\nL23@P1: by introducing a spatial separation between the active effector and the\nL24@P1: body part in which the touch is felt. This suggests that tactile attenuation is\nL25@P1: modulated by the degree to which the context of the action is consistent\nL26@P1: with self-generation. We discuss these findings with reference to the\nL27@P1: proposed purpose of sensory attenuation as a means of enhancing the\nL28@P1-2: salience of unexpected external events. Discriminating between self- and externally-generated sensation\nL29@P2: Whenever we move, speak or otherwise perform an action the resulting changes to\nL30@P2: our body and the environment are detected by our sensory systems. Under normal\nL31@P2: circumstances we recognise these sensations as the consequences of our actions and\nL32@P2: are able to distinguish them from similar sensations that are generated externally. We\nL33@P2: are unlikely to mistake the sound of another person talking for our own voice, and we\nL34@P2: have no difficulty distinguishing between movements we make ourselves and\nL35@P2: movements that are passively applied to our body by external forces. This ability to\nL36@P2: perceive our own actions as distinct from other people’s is an important part of our\nL37@P2: perception of ourselves as a single unified self, capable of willed action, and is\nL38@P2: probably crucial to our functioning as a social animal. The ability to discriminate\nL39@P2: between self- and externally-generated sensory input is also thought to play a role in\nL40@P2: some of the more fundamental functions of our sensory and motor systems.\nL41@P2: The earliest evidence for such a role came from the investigation of eye movements.\nL42@P2: When we move our eye the image of the world on our retina also moves, and yet we\nL43@P2: do not perceive the world to move. This is not simply because we have an expectation\nL44@P2: that the world will remain stable: as Descartes observed in his Treatise of Man (1664),\nL45@P2: tapping on the side of the eye with a fingertip generates an illusion of motion in the\nL46@P2: opposite direction, exactly as one would expect to occur during an eye movement. In\nL47@P2: the nineteenth century, inspired by Descartes’ observations, Helmholtz (1867)\nL48@P2: proposed that during normal eye movements the expected shift in the retinal image is\nL49@P2: compensated for in perception according to the ‘effort of will’ required to generate the\nL50@P2: movement. A century later, two separate studies both published in the same year\nL51@P2: proposed a model for this compensation (Sperry, 1950; von Holst and Mittelstaedt,\nL52@P2: 1950). According to von Holst’s ‘principle of reafference’, when the motor areas of\nL53@P2: the brain generate a motor command signal to move the eyes they also send a copy of\nL54@P2: the command to the visual areas. This ‘efference copy’ is used to generate a\nL55@P2: prediction of the change to the visual input that will result from the eye movement\nL56@P2: (termed the ‘corollary discharge’ by Sperry). This predicted shift in the visual input is\nL57@P2-3: then reversed and applied to the actual visual input at an early stage of processing, cancelling the real shift in the retinal image with the result that a stable percept of the\nL58@P3: world is maintained.\nL59@P3: The illusory movement observed by Descartes, Helmholtz and others when tapping on\nL60@P3: the side of the eye has a clear interpretation under von Holst’s model: the pressure\nL61@P3: applied by the finger causes a movement of the eye, but because this movement is not\nL62@P3: generated by the motor areas controlling the eye muscles an appropriate efference\nL63@P3: copy is not sent to the visual areas and the resulting retinal shift is not compensated\nL64@P3: for. As a result the world appears to move in the opposite direction to the applied\nL65@P3: force.\nL66@P3: Subsequent research has confirmed a role for efference copy in visual perception (e.g.\nL67@P3: Mach, 1885; Kornmuller, 1930; Mack and Bachant, 1969; Stevens et al., 1976)\nL68@P3: although a number of findings suggest that it may be only one of several mechanisms\nL69@P3: involved in maintaining visual stability (Bridgeman et al., 1975; Matin et al., 1982;\nL70@P3: Grusser et al., 1987; Pelz and Hayhoe, 1995). Nonetheless, the principle proposed by\nL71@P3: von Holst and Sperry of predicting the sensory consequences of action has been\nL72@P3: highly influential. Modern formulations of this theory (illustrated in Figure 1) include\nL73@P3: a forward model: an internal representation of the body and environment that is used\nL74@P3: to predict the consequences of a motor command (Jordan and Rumelhart, 1992; Miall\nL75@P3: and Wolpert, 1996).\nL76@P3: The predictions generated by forward models also have a number of other proposed\nL77@P3: uses within sensory and motor systems. A prediction of the way in which a motor\nL78@P3: command will change the state of the body and the environment is thought to underlie\nL79@P3: anticipatory motor control: for example, maintaining posture (Gahery and Massion,\nL80@P3: 1981; Massion, 1992) and generating appropriate grip forces when manipulating\nL81@P3: objects (Johansson and Cole, 1992; Flanagan and Wing, 1997). A prediction of the\nL82@P3: sensory feedback resulting from a motor command may not only underlie perceptual\nL83@P3: stability as in Von Holst’s model, but also be used in mental simulation (Sirigu et al.,\nL84@P3: 1996), in context estimation, and to compensate for inaccuracies and delays in sensory\nL85@P3-4: feedback (for a review see Davidson and Wolpert, 2005). Figure 1 Distinguishing between one’s own actions and external events. On the basis of\nL86@P4: efference copy, a forward model predicts the sensory feedback that will result from a\nL87@P4: planned action. The actual sensory feedback will reflect the sum of self- and externally\u0002generated changes to the body and environment. Subtracting the predicted from the\nL88@P4: actual sensory input reveals an estimate of the sensory feedback due to external\nL89@P4: influences.\nL90@P4: Sensory cancellation of tactile sensation\nL91@P4: As well as maintaining perceptual stability, it has been suggested that a cancellation\nL92@P4: mechanism of the kind illustrated in Figure 1 could also filter sensory input in order to\nL93@P4: help detect unexpected changes in our environment. By subtracting a proportion of\nL94@P4: the predicted sensory input from the actual input at an early stage of processing, self\u0002generated sensations could be attenuated, thereby enhancing the salience of\nL95@P4: unexpected external events. An everyday example of such attenuation may be found\nL96@P4: in the perception of tickle. It is a common experience that it is hard to tickle oneself,\nL97@P4: and empirical studies have confirmed that a self-generated tickle is perceived as less\nL98@P4: intense than an identical stimulus imposed externally (Weiskrantz et al., 1971;\nL99@P4-5: Claxton, 1975; Blakemore et al., 1998b). Several recent studies have shown that this phenomenon is not limited to tickling, but\nL100@P5: applies to the sense of touch in general. In a study by Shergill et al. (2003), a constant\nL101@P5: force was applied to a subject’s finger by a torque motor; subjects were then\nL102@P5: instructed to reproduce the force they had just felt by pressing with a finger of the\nL103@P5: other hand. Subjects consistently overestimated the force required (Figure 2, filled\nL104@P5: circles), implying that the sensation of force in the passive finger was perceived as\nL105@P5: substantially weaker when it was self-generated than when it was externally applied.\nL106@P5: This effect is not simply due to a failure of memory: the same subjects accurately\nL107@P5: reproduced the target force when they controlled the torque motor output with a\nL108@P5: joystick (Figure 2, empty circles). In this situation the active hand is not generating\nL109@P5: the force directly, but instead the movement of the hand is translated into a force via\nL110@P5: the torque motor. A study investigating the control of grip force (Blakemore et al.,\nL111@P5: 1998a) has shown that in this unusual situation predictive mechanisms are not\nL112@P5: employed. When one hand pushes on an object gripped in the other hand a precise\nL113@P5: anticipatory modulation of grip force is seen. However, when the force on the gripped\nL114@P5: object is instead controlled indirectly via a joystick, grip force modulation ceases to\nL115@P5: be predictive. In Shergill et al. (2003), the absence of attenuation when subjects\nL116@P5: reproduced the target force via the joystick is consistent with a similar failure of\nL117@P5-6: prediction. Figure 2 Matching force\nL118@P6: generated by subjects\nL119@P6: instructed to reproduce a\nL120@P6: target force applied to their\nL121@P6: index finger, either directly\nL122@P6: by pressing with the index\nL123@P6: finger of the other hand\nL124@P6: (filled circles, solid line) or\nL125@P6: indirectly via a joystick\nL126@P6: (empty circles, dotted line).\nL127@P6: Error bars indicate ±1 S.E.\nL128@P6: across subjects. Perfect\nL129@P6: performance is indicated by\nL130@P6: the dashed line. Adapted\nL131@P6: with permission from\nL132@P6: Shergill et al (2003).\nL133@P6: Sensory prediction deficits in schizophrenia\nL134@P6: The force-matching task described above has subsequently been used to test sensory\nL135@P6: prediction in patients with schizophrenia. As discussed, one role of sensory prediction\nL136@P6: may be to identify movements as either self- or externally-generated. If the predicted\nL137@P6: sensory input associated with a movement matches the actual sensory input, the\nL138@P6: movement is labelled as one’s own. However, if the predicted and actual sensory\nL139@P6: inputs are discordant, as when one’s arm is passively moved by someone else, the\nL140@P6: movement is labelled as externally-generated.\nL141@P6: If the mechanism that predicts the sensory consequences of action was dysfunctional\nL142@P6: and produced inaccurate predictions, this could cause the misattribution of self\u0002generated actions as externally-generated (Feinberg, 1978; Frith, 1992; Frith et al.,\nL143@P6-7: 2000). Many patients with schizophrenia demonstrate just such a deficit, in which self-generated actions are experienced as being under outside control or self-generated\nL144@P7: speech is misperceived as an auditory hallucination (Schneider, 1959).\nL145@P7: Shergill et al. (2005) used the force-matching task to directly test the hypothesis that\nL146@P7: patients with schizophrenia are defective in predicting the sensory consequences of\nL147@P7: their actions. Patients reproduced external forces substantially more accurately than\nL148@P7: age-matched control subjects (Figure 3), implying that the normal attenuation of the\nL149@P7: self-generated sensation was reduced in the schizophrenic patients. This study\nL150@P7: therefore provides strong evidence for a dysfunctional predictive mechanism in\nL151@P7: schizophrenia. As discussed above, efference copy signals can be used to generate\nL152@P7: both sensory and state predictions. State predictions are responsible for anticipatory\nL153@P7: motor control, which does not appear to be affected in schizophrenia (e.g. Delevoye\u0002Turrell et al., 2003). The deficit therefore seems to be specific to the sensory\nL154@P7: prediction of the consequences of action, as it is this sensory prediction that is\nL155@P7: implicated in both sensory attenuation and identification of self-action.\nL156@P7: Figure 3 Matching force\nL157@P7: generated using the right index\nL158@P7: finger (circles) and joystick\nL159@P7: (squares) as a function of the\nL160@P7: externally-generated target force,\nL161@P7: for patients (filled shapes) and\nL162@P7: healthy volunteers (empty\nL163@P7: shapes). Error bars indicate ±1\nL164@P7: SE across subjects. Dashed line\nL165@P7: represents perfect performance.\nL166@P7: Adapted with permission from\nL167@P7-8: Shergill et al (2005). Tactile attenuation is the result of a temporally-tuned predictive\nL168@P8: mechanism\nL169@P8: The mechanism of tactile attenuation has been further investigated by two studies\nL170@P8: which examined the perception of self-generated taps made by one finger on another.\nL171@P8: In Bays et al. (2005), subjects used their right index finger to tap a force sensor\nL172@P8: mounted above, but not in contact with, their left index finger. When a motor\nL173@P8: generated a tap on the left finger synchronous with the right tap, simulating contact\nL174@P8: between the fingers, the sensation of force in the left finger was attenuated compared\nL175@P8: to the same tap experienced during rest. By delaying or advancing the left tap relative\nL176@P8: to the active right tap, the time-course of this attenuation was mapped out, revealing a\nL177@P8: roughly symmetrical and relatively broad period of attenuation centred on the precise\nL178@P8-9: time at which the action would normally cause a tactile sensation (Figure 4 A). Figure 4 (A) Perceived magnitude of a\nL179@P9: tap made by the right index finger on the\nL180@P9: left as a function of the asynchrony\nL181@P9: between right finger contact and the tap\nL182@P9: on the left finger. Positive asynchrony\nL183@P9: indicates that right finger contact occurs\nL184@P9: first (i.e. the tap is delayed). Perceived\nL185@P9: magnitude was assessed by comparison\nL186@P9: with a reference tap delivered at rest.\nL187@P9: Adapted with permission from Bays et\nL188@P9: al. (2005). (B) Anticipatory modulation\nL189@P9: of grip force when dropping a ball into a\nL190@P9: grasped cup. Average load force (top)\nL191@P9: and grip force (bottom) on trials when\nL192@P9: the ball lands in the cup (dotted line) or\nL193@P9: is prevented from doing so (solid line).\nL194@P9: Abscissa shows time relative to the first\nL195@P9: peak in the mean load force due to\nL196@P9: impact (dashed line). Adapted with\nL197@P9: permission from Johansson and Westling\nL198@P9: (1988).\nL199@P9: There are interesting parallels between the results of this study and the findings of a\nL200@P9: grip force study which also investigated force pulses (Johansson and Westling, 1988).\nL201@P9: In this previous study, subjects dropped a ball from one hand into a cup supported in a\nL202@P9: precision grip by the other hand. The initial impact of the ball in the cup generated a\nL203@P9: brief force pulse similar to the taps used as test stimuli in the current study (dotted\nL204@P9: line, Figure 4 B top). To prevent the cup from slipping out of their grasp as a result of\nL205@P9: the impact, subjects increased their grip force around the time of contact.\nL206@P9: Occasionally, the experimenter prevented the dropped ball from hitting the cup,\nL207@P9: revealing a purely anticipatory component of the grip force modulation. The time\u0002course of this grip force increase (solid line, Figure 4 B bottom) bears a number of\nL208@P9: similarities to the time-course of attenuation seen in the current study: it has a similar\nL209@P9: temporal width, is roughly symmetrical, and is centred on the expected time of the\nL210@P9-10: initial force peak. These similarities are consistent with the hypothesis that tactile attenuation, like grip force modulation, depends on a prediction of the consequences\nL211@P10: of action generated by a forward model. In both cases the time profile is considerably\nL212@P10: broader than the actual duration of the force pulse. This could reflect inaccuracy or\nL213@P10: uncertainty of the internal model in predicting the time of the contact event, or a\nL214@P10: ‘safety margin’ built into the attenuation and grip force systems to allow for the\nL215@P10: possibility of a prediction error.\nL216@P10: While the results of the psychophysical studies of attenuation described above are\nL217@P10: consistent with a predictive mechanism, they are equally consistent with a\nL218@P10: reconstructive or postdictive mechanism. A postdictive mechanism is one in which\nL219@P10: the percept of a sensory event is constructed from sensory information received\nL220@P10: around the time of the event (Dennett and Kinsbourne, 1992; Eagleman and\nL221@P10: Sejnowski, 2000; Rao et al., 2001). In this mechanism the original sensory input is\nL222@P10: available for a period after the event and its processing can depend substantially on\nL223@P10: other events that occur in close temporal proximity. Bays et al. (2006) found strong\nL224@P10: evidence to suggest that attenuation of self-generated tactile sensation results from a\nL225@P10: predictive, not postdictive, mechanism. When one finger made a tapping movement\nL226@P10: above a finger of the other hand, sensation in the passive finger was attenuated only\nL227@P10: when contact was expected between the fingers. Furthermore, the level of attenuation\nL228@P10: observed when contact was expected was the same whether or not the contact actually\nL229@P10: occurred. These results are inconsistent with a postdictive mechanism, which would\nL230@P10: have access to the actual sensory feedback in determining the level of attenuation, and\nL231@P10: hence confirm that tactile attenuation results from a prediction of the sensory\nL232@P10: consequences of action.\nL233@P10: Here we conduct two new experiments to examine in more detail the mechanism\nL234@P10: underlying tactile attenuation. In addition we perform a meta-analysis of data from a\nL235@P10: number of studies that have used the force-matching task.\nL236@P10-11: Experiment 1: Spatial and Magnitude Influences on Attenuation A previous study of predictive motor control has shown that accurate prediction\nL237@P11: requires a natural correspondence between actions and their sensory consequences\nL238@P11: (Blakemore et al., 1998a). Two robot arms were used to simulate holding an object\nL239@P11: between the hands. As with a real object, when subjects tried to move the object with\nL240@P11: their left hand it caused a force to be transmitted to the right hand. Subjects generated\nL241@P11: an anticipatory increase in grip force in the right hand to prevent the object from\nL242@P11: slipping. For the simulation of a real object to be complete, force feedback must in\nL243@P11: turn be transmitted back to the left hand, resisting its movement. When the\nL244@P11: experimenters changed the gain of this force feedback, making the context less\nL245@P11: consistent with a real object, the anticipatory grip-force response was diminished.\nL246@P11: This suggests that the motor prediction underlying grip-force modulation depends on\nL247@P11: a realistic relationship between force input and output. Similarly, predictive tactile\nL248@P11: attenuation may require a realistic correspondence between force-generation and\nL249@P11: sensation.\nL250@P11: To investigate this possibility, we conducted an experiment in which subjects\nL251@P11: reproduced target forces applied to their finger by pressing through a virtual object,\nL252@P11: simulated by two torque motors. This allowed the force generated by the active right\nL253@P11: finger to be dissociated from the force delivered to the passive left finger. We used\nL254@P11: this dissociation firstly to manipulate the spatial co-alignment between active and\nL255@P11: passive fingers. If predictive attenuation requires a realistic spatial correspondence\nL256@P11: between force input and output, introducing a horizontal separation between the\nL257@P11: fingers should decrease the level of attenuation observed in the matching task.\nL258@P11: Secondly, we manipulated the gain: doubling or halving the force transmitted from\nL259@P11: the active finger to the passive finger. If predictive attenuation requires that the force\nL260@P11: generated by the active finger be equivalent to the force experienced in the passive\nL261@P11: finger, adjusting the gain will reduce the level of attenuation.\nL262@P11: Methods\nL263@P11: After providing written informed consent 16 right-handed subjects (9 male, 7 female,\nL264@P11: aged 18-40) participated in the experiment. The experimental protocol was approved\nL265@P11: by a local ethics committee. Each subject rested his or her left index finger in a\nL266@P11: moulded support. A force sensor (Nano-17 6-axis F/T sensor, ATI Inc.) rested on the\nL267@P11-12: tip of the finger at the end of a lever attached to a torque motor (Maxon Motors UK, Model RE35; geared in ratio 1:4.8). The motor was fitted with a rotary optical\nL268@P12: encoder (Incremental Encoders Direct Ltd, Model SA40). To start each trial the\nL269@P12: torque motor applied a constant target force to the tip of the subject’s index finger for\nL270@P12: 3 s. Following an auditory go-signal, subjects were required to reproduce the force\nL271@P12: they had just felt by pressing with the index finger of the other hand. After 3 s an\nL272@P12: auditory stop-signal was given to end the trial. Each subject completed five\nL273@P12: consecutive experimental conditions in a pseudorandom order, each consisting of fifty\nL274@P12: trials: ten trials each of five target forces in the range 1 N to 3 N.\nL275@P12: Subjects generated the matching force on their left index finger indirectly, via a\nL276@P12: virtual link between two torque motors. Condition 1 was designed to simulate the\nL277@P12: direct generation of force as closely as possible. In order to produce the matching\nL278@P12: force, subjects pressed with their right index finger on a second force sensor situated\nL279@P12: directly above the first (Figure 5 A). This force (recorded online at 1000 Hz and\nL280@P12: smoothed with a 15 point mean filter) was transmitted to the left index finger by the\nL281@P12: lower torque motor. Because the surface of the fingertip yields under pressure,\nL282@P12: applying a force led to a small downward deflection of the lower lever, which was\nL283@P12: recorded by the rotary encoder fitted to the lower torque motor. In order to maintain\nL284@P12: an accurate simulation of a virtual object between the fingers, the upper torque motor\nL285@P12: adjusted the position of the upper force sensor online so as to maintain a constant\nL286@P12: vertical distance between the force sensors. It was previously explained to subjects\nL287@P12: that there would be no physical object between the fingers but that forces they\nL288@P12: produced would be transmitted to the other finger via the computer.\nL289@P12: Conditions 2 and 3 were identical to condition 1 except that the upper torque motor\nL290@P12: was re-positioned, so that during force generation the left and right fingertips were\nL291@P12: separated laterally by a distance of 10 cm (condition 2) or 30 cm (condition 3).\nL292@P12: Conditions 4 and 5 were again identical to condition 1 except that now we adjusted\nL293@P12: the gain relationship between the fingers, such that a 1 N force applied by the right\nL294@P12: finger resulted in a 0.5 N (condition 5) or a 2 N (condition 6) force on the left finger.\nL295@P12: The matching force level generated on the passive finger was calculated for each trial\nL296@P12: by taking the mean force recorded by the lower force sensor between 2000 and 2500\nL297@P12-13: ms after the go-signal. Results\nL298@P13: In order to investigate some of the parameters that might affect sensory attenuation, in\nL299@P13: this experiment we created a dissociation between force input and output. The control\nL300@P13: condition (condition 1) simulated as closely as possible direct force-generation by the\nL301@P13: right index finger on the left, as in Shergill et al. (2003). As in the previous study,\nL302@P13: subjects applied substantially more force than was required to reproduce the target\nL303@P13: force (Figure 5 B, empty circles). This greater matching force was perceived by\nL304@P13: subjects as equal to the target force because a proportion of the self-generated\nL305@P13: sensation was attenuated.\nL306@P13: Figure 5 (A) Schematic of the experimental apparatus. Forces applied to the upper force\nL307@P13: sensor were transmitted with a variable gain to the left index finger via the lower torque\nL308@P13: motor. The upper torque motor could be moved in a direction lateral to the subject to\nL309@P13: introduce a spatial separation between the active and passive fingers. (B) Mean\nL310@P13: matching force generated by subjects with lateral separations between active and\nL311@P13: passive fingers of 0 cm (empty circles), 10 cm (diamonds) and 30 cm (squares). (C) Mean\nL312@P13: matching force generated by subjects with gains of 0.5 (downward triangles), 1.0 (empty\nL313@P13: circles), and 2.0 (upward triangles). Error bars indicate ±1 SE across subjects. Dashed\nL314@P13: line represents perfect performance.\nL315@P13: Having created a dissociation between force input and output we were able to\nL316@P13: investigate the effect of spatial separation on attenuation. Introducing a 10 or 30 cm\nL317@P13-14: lateral separation between the active right and passive left fingers resulted in a reduction in the matching force level compared to the control condition, implying a\nL318@P14: reduced level of attenuation (Figure 5 B, filled shapes). A two-way ANOVA\nL319@P14: (separation distance x target force) revealed a significant effect of separation on\nL320@P14: matching force level (F2,30 = 5.5, p = 0.009). Post-hoc tests found no significant\nL321@P14: differences between the 10 and 30 cm separations (t15 = 0.17, p = 0.87) but significant\nL322@P14: differences of both from the no separation condition (t15 > 2.5, p < 0.023). However,\nL323@P14: subjects still significantly over-estimated the matching force required at both 10 and\nL324@P14: 30 cm separations (t15 > 3.66, p < 0.003), implying that attenuation was not entirely\nL325@P14: abolished.\nL326@P14: The effect on attenuation of varying the gain relationship between the fingers is\nL327@P14: shown in Figure 5 C. In three conditions the gain was adjusted such that each newton\nL328@P14: of force applied by the right finger resulted in a 0.5 N, 1 N, or 2 N force on the left\nL329@P14: finger. A two-way ANOVA (gain x target force) revealed no significant effect of gain\nL330@P14: on matching force level (F2,30 = 0.23, p = 0.79).\nL331@P14: Discussion\nL332@P14: It has been suggested that the attenuation of self-generated sensation described in this\nL333@P14: chapter may have evolved in order to increase the salience of externally-generated\nL334@P14: sensation. To be effective therefore the underlying mechanism must be able to\nL335@P14: correctly identify when two parts of the body are interacting and apply attenuation\nL336@P14: only in such situations. This judgement is likely to be based on a range of factors,\nL337@P14: some of which we have attempted to identify in Experiment 1. In order to do this we\nL338@P14: simulated normal force-generation using a virtual link between two torque motors.\nL339@P14: Substantial attenuation was still observed even though subjects were made aware that\nL340@P14: there was no physical object between the fingers. However, when we introduced a\nL341@P14: lateral spatial separation between the fingers, the level of sensory attenuation was\nL342@P14: reduced. This suggests that a spatial co-alignment between force production and\nL343@P14: sensation may be one of the factors by which sensations are identified as self\u0002generated. When force production and sensation are not aligned it reduces the\nL344@P14: confidence with which the sensation can be identified as self- rather than externally\u0002generated and so less attenuation is applied. Although infrequent, spatial\nL345@P14-15: misalignments in force of the size examined in this study can occur during manipulation of large objects, and this may explain why the attenuation is reduced but\nL346@P15: not abolished.\nL347@P15: Probably the clearest evidence that two parts of the body are interacting is a precise\nL348@P15: correspondence between the force generated by one body-part and the force felt in the\nL349@P15: other at the same moment. Consistent with this, it has been shown that introducing a\nL350@P15: temporal asynchrony between activity and tactile sensation reduces sensory\nL351@P15: attenuation (Blakemore et al., 1999; Bays et al., 2005). However, in this study we\nL352@P15: have demonstrated that altering the gain relationship between the fingers does not\nL353@P15: affect the level of attenuation. Specifically, doubling or halving the force transmitted\nL354@P15: from the active finger to the passive finger did not alter the extent to which sensation\nL355@P15: in the passive finger was attenuated. This result suggests that while temporal\nL356@P15: correlation between force-generation and sensation may be required to elicit sensory\nL357@P15: attenuation, an equal magnitude of force does not appear to be important. This is\n"
